    location: "Family dining area — last week home",
    narration: "Breakfast is loud and crowded. Her kuya has already stopped by before his hospital shift. Her tita from next door brought bibingka. Two cousins are arguing about basketball. Nobody was called. Everyone just came. Marites' grandmother grew up in a barangay where Bayanihan was not a concept — it was a physical event. When a family needed to move their bahay kubo — their bamboo home raised on stilts — the entire village would gather unannounced, crouch beneath the structure together, and carry the whole house to its new location on their collective shoulders. Fifteen, twenty men. No one appointed, no one paid. Lifting and moving because the need was visible and the community was the response. When it was done, the family prepared food. That was the entire exchange. Today we carry yours. Tomorrow you carry ours. That is Bayanihan. Not a feeling. A physical act. A village that can lift a house when one family cannot. Marites sits down and her plate is filled before she asks. Her mother says: Kumain ka na — eat now — meaning: you take care of everyone else, so let us take care of you. Nobody cries when the conversation turns to departure. They problem-solve. Her kuya: Keep a printed copy of your license. Do not trust only digital. Her tita: Call us Sundays, we will all be home. Her mother: Eat rice. Even over there. In U.S. healthcare workplaces, Bayanihan instinct can create friction. Unsolicited help across role boundaries can be received as overstepping or scope confusion. Marites will need to translate this instinct — offer support through sanctioned channels, coordinate rather than self-deploy. The spirit stays. The execution adapts.",
