    narration: "On the living room wall, there is still a parol — the star-shaped Christmas lantern — left from the holiday season. In the Philippines, Christmas begins in September. Not metaphorically. The radio stations switch to carols. The malls put up lights. Families begin planning who will host Noche Buena — the midnight Christmas Eve feast. By December, Simbang Gabi has started — nine consecutive dawn Masses, attended even by people who sleep through the rest of the year. Marites has not missed a single one since she was seven. She pauses at the parol before picking up her bag. She knows she will not be home for the ber months. She will not be at Noche Buena. She will not hear her family sing during Simbang Gabi. She tucks that away somewhere quiet and keeps moving. Workers who migrate for caregiving roles carry invisible emotional burdens. Strong organizational inclusion practices — not just policy posters — create environments where that burden is not compounded by workplace exclusion.",
