    narration: "At her clinic in Quezon City, Marites is the nurse colleagues ask for when a patient interaction is going wrong. Today she is teaching wound care to an elderly patient whose daughter is translating. Marites stops partway through and says gently: Nay, pwede bang ulitin mo sa akin? — Can you repeat that back to me? The patient hesitates, then describes the steps back — correctly, but with one small inversion. Marites does not say wrong. She smiles and says: Almost — let me show you that part once more. When the daughter tries to shortcut and says she got it, Marites says calmly: I just want to make sure for her safety. One more minute. She documents exactly what was explained, what the patient repeated back, and what still needs reinforcement. This is teach-back. It is not a formality. It is the verification mechanism that closes the gap between what was said and what was understood. The wrong answer to whether the patient understood is she nodded. The right answer is a documented description of what the patient demonstrated or repeated back.",
