    narration: "Marites never addresses a patient without using po — the Tagalog softening particle that signals respect toward elders and authority. It is so automatic she does not notice it anymore. She calls older women Ma'am or Ate — older sister, even when they are strangers. She calls men Kuya or Sir. She steps back to let elders through doorways. She waits to be seated before sitting. These are not performance behaviors. They are the grammar of respect she was raised speaking. She will arrive in the U.S. and find that some of these signals land differently. A patient might interpret her deference as uncertainty. A colleague might read her careful listening as passivity. A supervisor might not recognize her soft-spoken precision as confidence. She will need to translate not just language, but communication register — and she will need to do it without losing the core values underneath. Every culture has a distinct communication register. Assuming a shared communication code creates patient safety risk.",
