    narration: "The airport in the United States is large, efficient, and impersonal. Nobody helps without being asked first. Nobody makes eye contact unless they need something. The signage is clear. The process is fast. Nobody lingers. In the Philippines, airports are loud with reunions. Here, arrivals walk briskly and separately. She takes the bus to her temporary housing. The driver does not respond to her greeting. Not rudely — he just does not respond. She files that away. Different system. Her first evening: she unpacks, eats a Cup Noodles because the grocery run can wait, and calls her family on video chat. Her grandmother is already asleep. Her mother holds the phone up anyway so she can see the parol still on the wall. She keeps the video open until the data connection drops. Culture shock is not weakness. It is the cognitive load of recalibrating every social and professional assumption simultaneously. For healthcare workers, unmanaged culture shock can affect communication quality, documentation accuracy, and patient relationship-building.",
