    title: "First Week: The System Is Different",
    location: "Agency — nursing station and EHR workstation",
    narration: "The EHR system has twelve required fields she has never seen. The abbreviations are different from the ones she trained on. The handoff format is faster and more verbal — less written, more assumed. In the Philippines, a ward handoff included written notes, verbal confirmation, and physical bedside assessment. Here, the expectation is a ten-minute verbal briefing and a chart review. The first time she asks a clarifying question during handoff, the charge nurse glances at the clock. Not unkindly — but the signal is clear: brevity is currency here. She writes everything down. She stays an extra twenty minutes reviewing charts before leaving. She does not ask questions she can answer herself. She saves her questions for the ones that matter. Adapting to a faster handoff culture does not mean abandoning closed-loop communication. It means executing it efficiently. One targeted clarifying question, timed correctly, is more valuable than ten vague ones at the wrong moment.",
