    title: "First Grocery Store and Small Shocks",
    location: "Local grocery store — off day",
    narration: "She goes to the grocery store on her day off. She spends twenty minutes looking for garlic rice. She cannot find it. She settles for white rice and garlic from the produce section. She brings everything to the cashier and says: Good morning, Ma'am — and the cashier, without looking up, says: You have a rewards card? Not rude. Just transactional. In the Philippines, strangers at market stalls ask where you are going, who your family is, whether you have eaten. The cashier is doing her job efficiently. Marites is grieving a style of warmth she did not know she needed. She buys her groceries and walks back to her apartment. She texts her lola a photo of the white rice. Her lola responds with three rice emojis and a laughing face. She laughs alone in her apartment and feels slightly better. Clinical relationships in American home health are professional and boundaried — not cold, but structured. Knowing this difference allows the clinician to adapt without misreading professional distance as hostility.",
