    id: "M01-S13",
    type: "content",
    title: "Resilience in Practice",
    location: "Agency — break room and hallway",
    narration: "In the Philippines, Marites has worked through a typhoon warning, a dengue outbreak in her barangay, and a hospital generator failure during a night shift. She kept working. She figured out what was needed and she did it. Filipino resilience — the cultural norm of maintaining a positive face and continuing to function under pressure — is not denial. It is a survival skill that her community practices so consistently it becomes professional reflex. Here, she is watching colleagues express frustration openly — at the system, at the scheduler, at the EHR — in ways she was trained to absorb quietly. She notices that here, speaking up is not the same as complaining. It is sometimes how the system gets fixed. She is building a new operational vocabulary, and she is doing it deliberately. Compliance and safety culture in ACHC-accredited agencies depend on staff who report concerns, document anomalies, and escalate without waiting for permission. Speaking up is not disloyalty — it is the job.",
