    location: "Agency conference room — orientation day",
    narration: "At agency orientation, Marites is given a thick binder. She reads it the way she prepared for licensing exams — front to back, with a highlighter. She marks: Language Access. Interpreter Services Required. Patient Rights. Anti-Discrimination. Reporting Obligations. Anti-Retaliation. In her Philippine clinic, many of these existed as values rather than written operational standards. Here they are legal obligations with documented workflows, survey traceability, and corrective action pathways. The commitment is the same. The architecture is more formal. She reads the Anti-Retaliation clause twice. She has never worked somewhere that explicitly wrote down that she could not be punished for reporting a concern. She finds this remarkable. ACHC accreditation requires that agencies not only have these policies but demonstrate evidence that staff understand and apply them. Orientation is the beginning of audit-traceable competency documentation. Staff who read carefully and ask questions are building defensibility.",
