    narration: "In the hallway outside the patient coordination room, there is a laminated board. Patient rights. Language access. Interpreter services at no charge. Anti-discrimination protections. Grievance process. Marites stops and reads it as if she has never seen policies before — because here, they are the operational landscape she will navigate daily. She notices: interpreter services are explicitly required, not suggested. Family members may not substitute for qualified interpretation in clinical decisions. This is different from what she saw in her previous work environment, where family interpretation was common practice driven by necessity. She photographs the board on her personal phone. Not for evidence — for study. The policies posted in an ACHC-accredited agency are operational standards surveyors will evaluate during review. Every field worker is expected to know them, apply them, document them, and escalate when they are not followed.",
