    title: "Challenge 1: Cultural Preference vs Clinical Urgency — Question",
    location: "Mr. Henderson home — front door",
    narration: "Help Marites make the correct operational decision. She has arrived at Mr. Henderson's home for a wound care visit. The family meets her at the door and tells her their religious beliefs require gender-concordant care — Marites cannot provide the treatment. She looks at the wound dressing. It is soiled. The wound may be deteriorating. The patient cannot speak to confirm or deny the accommodation request. Marites has four options. What is the most defensible immediate action? Option A: Acknowledge the family's request, rapidly assess wound urgency at the bedside, call the supervisor immediately for accommodation routing, and document both the preference and the clinical findings. Option B: Respect the family's preference completely by leaving the home and messaging the office to reschedule with a different clinician. Option C: Proceed with wound care because clinical urgency overrides accommodation requests when deterioration is present. Option D: Ask the family to sign a refusal form first, then determine next steps based on available staffing.",
    topic: "Cultural awareness; Patient rights; Escalation",
